al: Belknap Press / Harvard University Press"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.28,"confidence":"low","evidence":["academic press signal: Belknap Press / Harvard University Press"]},{"id":"argument_driven","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"theory\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Rawls argues that principles of justice are those free and rational people would choose in an initial position of equality behind a veil of ignorance.","confidence":0.62},"academicOrientation":{"score":76,"confidence":0.55},"profileConfidence":0.78,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"87b82e0de085b4688143a28c7493358ece96e4312647c0098cc7e8e3dc473c93","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Politics & Government","secondarySubjects":[],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores theories of justice within the contexts of political philosophy and ethics, making it most suitable under Politics & Government.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"56674958216420a1867c6235e2681d9498e1136fe6847cf652202fbe5537f5bb","summary":"The principles of justice Rawls set forth in this book are those that free and rational people would accept in an initial position of equality. In this hypothetical situation, which corresponds to the state of nature in social contract theory, no one knows his or her place in society; his or her class position or social status; his or her fortune in the distribution of natural assets and abilities; his or her intelligence, strength, and the like; or even his or her conception of the good. Thus, deliberating behind a veil of ignorance, people determine their rights and duties. The first section of A Theory of Justice addresses objections to the theory and discusses alternative positions, especially utilitarianism. Rawls then applies his theory to the philosophical basis of constitutional liberties, the problem of distributive justice, and the grounds and limits of political duty and obligation. He includes here a discussion of civil disobedience and conscientious objection. Finally, he connects his theory of justice with a doctrine of the good and of moral development. This enables him to formulate a conception of society as a social union of social unions, and to use his theory of justice to explain the values of community. Since its first appearance in 1971, A Theory of Justice has been continuously taught and debated, and translated into twenty-four languages.","publicationYear":1971,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Justice","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-theory-of-justice-john-rawls"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Philosophy","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-theory-of-justice-john-rawls"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Ethics","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-theory-of-justice-john-rawls"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Law","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-theory-of-justice-john-rawls"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Political science","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-theory-of-justice-john-rawls"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Social Justice","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-theory-of-justice-john-rawls"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Rechtvaardigheid","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-theory-of-justice-john-rawls"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Gerechtigkeit","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-theory-of-justice-john-rawls"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Rechts